<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="KitveiHakodeshService.app"/>
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <!--
          asInvoker — deliberately NOT requireAdministrator.

          Administrator rights are a pure OPTIMIZATION here, never a requirement:
            - NTFS USN journal access (FSCTL_READ_USN_JOURNAL, FSCTL_ENUM_USN_DATA)
              gives PathIndex a fast MFT index build + live file-system updates.
          Both degrade cleanly when they're unavailable:
            - PathIndex.Build falls back to FallbackCrawler (directory walk), which
              indexes the same extensions and skips the same folders, so the index is
              equivalent — just slower to build the first time.
            - PathIndex.StartLiveUpdates pre-flights UsnJournal.CanOpenVolume and exits
              silently when the volume handle is refused; searches keep working against
              the built index, they just don't see files created while we run.

          requireAdministrator was tried and REVERTED: a hard elevation request makes
          CreateProcess fail outright with ERROR_ELEVATION_REQUIRED (-4094) from any
          non-elevated parent. That broke `npm run dev` from a normal terminal — vite
          spawns this exe, the spawn failed, so /khs-endpoint 503'd forever and the whole
          dev data path was dead unless the terminal itself was restarted elevated.
          asInvoker means we inherit the parent's token: elevated parent → USN fast path
          and live updates; normal parent → fallback crawl. Either way we always START.
        -->
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false"/>
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 / 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
